<p>I recently obtained my PhD in <a href="https://aos.princeton.edu">Atmospheric &amp; Oceanic Sciences</a> from <a href="https://www.princeton.edu">Princeton University</a>, where I was advised by <a href="https://stephengriffies.github.io/">Dr. Stephen Griffies</a>. Prior to attending Princeton University, I obtained a BSc in Mathematical Physics from the <a href="https://www.ualberta.ca">University of Alberta</a> where I worked with <a href="https://sites.ualberta.ca/~bsuther/">Prof. Bruce Sutherland</a>.</p>

<p>My most recent work is on a <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JPO-D-22-0040.1">quasi-2D turbulence regime</a> that is relevant for rotating stratified fluids (e.g., planetary atmospheres or oceans). I created a new theoretical model for this class of turbulence and I developed a theory for how energy is distributed across spatial scales. In addition, I have used this model to investigate the formation of <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.03155">anisotropic structures</a> arising from the interaction of waves with turbulence. This work is likely to be relevant for the upcoming <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surface_Water_and_Ocean_Topography">SWOT satellite</a> mission which will provide data on upper ocean turbulence at an unprecedented resolution. More details can be found <a href="/research">here</a>.</p>

<p>I also have more mathematical work on the <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0048273">Fourier analysis of geophysical waves</a>. This work describes the mathematical properties of Fourier expansions for systems for dynamically-active boundaries. Such systems are common in geophysical fluids (e.g., surface gravity waves, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/JPO-D-21-0199.1">Rossby edge waves</a>, capillary waves).</p>

<p>Finally, I have also worked on the <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/jfm.2019.30">transport</a> induced by <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/jfm.2022.690">internal gravity waves</a>. This transport is analogous to the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stokes_drift">Stokes drift</a> induced surface gravity waves, but occurs throughout the volume of the fluid.</p>

<p>My work can be found <a href="/publications">here</a>. You can watch animations of the turbulent systems I study <a href="/research">here</a>. You can reach me at <a href="mailto:houssam@ualberta.edu"><em>houssam</em>@<strong>ualberta</strong>.ca</a>.</p>

